--TEST--
A shape field written `name?:` may be absent from the value, and the fields that are present still read correctly — `rule:types/shape-type`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:types/shape-type`: the `?` before the `:` relaxes presence of the key
// and nothing else. A value carrying only the required field satisfies the
// shape.
({a?: int, x: int}) $p = {x: 1};
echo $p->x, "\n";

// The optional field is deliberately first in the shape's sorted field order
// and absent from the value, so the shape's own field position for `x` is not
// the value's. The fetch is name-keyed for exactly this reason, so it still
// answers 1 above.

// Present, and then checked like any other field.
({a?: int, x: int}) $q = {a: 7, x: 2};
echo $q->a, " ", $q->x, "\n";

// Width subtyping still applies on top of it: an extra field the shape does
// not name is no problem either.
({a?: int, x: int}) $r = {x: 3, z: "extra"};
echo $r->x, "\n";
--EXPECT--
1
7 2
3
